Report a missing relative file operand as not found, not fatally.
Before changing to the system drive, man made each file operand absolute
with xabsolute_path(), which resolves a relative path with realpath()
and dies when that fails. One missing file such as "man ./x.1 ./y.1"
therefore stopped man with "can't open './x.1'" before it showed any
page, while a missing absolute path was reported as "no manual entry"
and the other operands were processed. Make file operands absolute with
_fullpath(), which does not require the file to exist, so that a
missing file is reported like any other page that is not found.

---
 miscutils/man.c |    8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/miscutils/man.c
+++ b/miscutils/man.c
@@ -313,8 +313,12 @@
 #if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
 	/* add system drive prefix to filenames, if necessary */
 	for (ptr = argv; *ptr; ++ptr) {
-		if (strchr(*ptr, '/') || strchr(*ptr, '\\'))
-			*ptr = bs_to_slash(xabsolute_path(*ptr));
+		if (strchr(*ptr, '/') || strchr(*ptr, '\\')) {
+			/* unlike xabsolute_path() this works for a missing
+			 * file, which is then reported as not found */
+			char *path = _fullpath(NULL, *ptr, 0);
+			*ptr = bs_to_slash(path ? path : xabsolute_path(*ptr));
+		}
 	}
 	cwd = xrealloc_getcwd_or_warn(NULL);
 	xchdir_system_drive();
